ID animal-kinds
OLD {"id": "animal-kinds", "label": "Animals grouped by shared features", "description": "Comparison makes similarities and differences available to inquiry."}
NEW {"id": "animal-kinds", "label": "Animals compared by structures and activities", "description": "Shared parts and their different activities provide a concrete basis for comparison; not every similarity establishes one natural group."}
ID atoms-void
OLD {"id": "atoms-void", "label": "Epicurus: bodies move in the void", "description": "A reconstructed material account, not modern atomic physics."}
NEW {"id": "atoms-void", "label": "Epicurus: mind and body are made of dispersible atoms", "description": "A reconstructed Epicurean material account, not modern atomic physics. The soul is bodily and its dispersal removes sensation; this is part of the ancient argument about fear, not a claim the player must endorse."}
ID automated-workshop
OLD {"id": "automated-workshop", "label": "Imitation and machinery enter the workshop", "description": "An earlier distinction between performed motions and life now helps identify what automation does—and does not replace."}
NEW {}
ID automation-with-agency
OLD {"id": "automation-with-agency", "label": "Automation leaves room for makers’ decisions", "description": "Speculative synthesis keeps reduced toil and control over the work as separate design requirements."}
NEW {"id": "automation-with-agency", "label": "A workshop design reserves decisions for its makers", "description": "Speculative institutional design joins labour-saving tools to specified task-setting and pause rights. It does not infer meaningful agency from community ownership alone or guarantee successful enforcement."}
ID blood-circuit
OLD {"id": "blood-circuit", "label": "Blood in a circulating system", "description": "Heart action and directional pathways form a circulation account, not a sequence of volume sums."}
NEW {"id": "blood-circuit", "label": "Out through arteries, back through veins", "description": "Reconstruct Harvey’s outward-and-return circulation account. The connecting passage through tissues remained unobserved by him; this is not a complete modern vascular diagram."}
ID cave-shadows
OLD {"id": "cave-shadows", "label": "Prisoners take shadows for the whole of reality", "description": "A paraphrase of Plato’s cave image; the player needs no unseen diagram."}
NEW {"id": "cave-shadows", "label": "A freed prisoner turns from shadows toward their sources", "description": "Plato’s cave image contrasts habitual shadow-taking with a difficult reorientation toward what produces the appearances. This paraphrased case supplies the turning, not an unseen diagram."}
ID changed-meaning-of-kinds
OLD {"id": "changed-meaning-of-kinds", "label": "A species name does not freeze its history", "description": "The earlier changing-species proposal remains distinct from the newer selection mechanism."}
NEW {"id": "changed-meaning-of-kinds", "label": "Changing species, different accounts of how", "description": "Lamarck’s transforming species and Darwin–Wallace selection both reject simple fixity, but they are not the same mechanism or complete historical theory. Their comparison keeps the disagreement visible rather than merely renaming species change."}
ID clockwork-figure
OLD {"id": "clockwork-figure", "label": "An animated clockwork figure", "description": "The mechanism moves a lifelike figure; the inherited device is a teaching comparison, not a claimed continuous artifact lineage."}
NEW {"id": "clockwork-figure", "label": "A figure moved by a copied gear linkage", "description": "Modern teaching comparison: adapt a linkage represented in the earlier model into the supplied figure and gears. The retained sky model does not mysteriously power the new object, and no direct ancient-to-modern transmission is claimed."}
ID control-of-work
OLD {"id": "control-of-work", "label": "Less effort can mean less control", "description": "Keep a genuine trade-off alive: reducing toil and retaining agency are separate design demands."}
NEW {"id": "control-of-work", "label": "Mechanized effort can deepen lost control", "description": "The existing loss of control over work meets the concrete case of a machine-set pace. Saving physical labour does not guarantee control over tempo or decisions."}
ID convention-questioned
OLD {"id": "convention-questioned", "label": "Convention is not automatically nature", "description": "A provocative life can expose customs that present themselves as necessities."}
NEW {"id": "convention-questioned", "label": "Local respectability does not settle a good life", "description": "The earlier demand to examine how one lives now tests a concrete rival standard: honourable custom varies between cities. Convention cannot settle the question merely by claiming local approval."}
ID cross-checked-discovery
OLD {"id": "cross-checked-discovery", "label": "A candidate survives more than one instrument", "description": "A possible discovery earns support through distinct checks; the game does not claim a real future scientific result."}
NEW {"id": "cross-checked-discovery", "label": "A fictional candidate survives distinct checks", "description": "The proposed inquiry receives stipulated agreeing observations and calibration checks. These support the fictional candidate without guaranteeing truth or asserting that a real future discovery occurred."}
ID cynic-sufficiency
OLD {"id": "cynic-sufficiency", "label": "Freedom by needing less", "description": "Reconstruct Cynic self-sufficiency as a practical response to the question of a good life."}
NEW {"id": "cynic-sufficiency", "label": "Freedom by needing less", "description": "Diogenes’s reduced dependence meets a ruler’s offer directly: fewer wants can make patronage less coercive. This reconstructed ideal does not imply that involuntary poverty is harmless."}
ID debt-objection
OLD {"id": "debt-objection", "label": "Repayment can do harm", "description": "Returning the weapon exposes a difficulty in treating repayment as the whole of justice."}
NEW {"id": "debt-objection", "label": "Repayment can do harm", "description": "The stated repayment definition meets a concrete counterexample directly: returning the weapon can harm its owner or others. It is no longer necessary to rename the definition first."}
ID deep-time-world
OLD {"id": "deep-time-world", "label": "A landscape needs a deep past", "description": "Repeated large changes make a vastly extended history intelligible before there is an accurate numerical age."}
NEW {"id": "deep-time-world", "label": "A landscape needs a deep past", "description": "Repeated deposition, deformation and erosion across a correlated rock record support an extended history. The geographically developed succession is not rejected in favour of a less-developed local version; no exact duration has yet been measured."}
ID epicurean-nature
OLD {"id": "epicurean-nature", "label": "An Epicurean material world", "description": "A different ancient natural account serves a different way of living; no direct influence from this particular earlier case is asserted."}
NEW {}
ID error-correcting-message
OLD {"id": "error-correcting-message", "label": "A message designed to survive some errors", "description": "The useful construction changes the representation, not the underlying message’s truth or meaning."}
NEW {"id": "error-correcting-message", "label": "A message designed to recover from specified errors", "description": "The stipulated correction scheme changes the representation so that the assumed error patterns can be located and corrected. It does not repair every possible corruption or establish the message’s truth."}
ID factory-speed
OLD {"id": "factory-speed", "label": "An automatic machine can dictate the pace", "description": "Labour-saving for one person may mean less control for another."}
NEW {"id": "factory-speed", "label": "A labour-saving machine dictates the worker’s pace", "description": "Saving physical effort can coexist with less control over tempo and working conditions."}
ID frame-dependent-simultaneity
OLD {"id": "frame-dependent-simultaneity", "label": "Different frames need not agree on distant simultaneity", "description": "With the same light speed used in each inertial frame, their synchronized clocks do not supply one frame-independent distant present. No arithmetic or unseen diagram is required."}
NEW {"id": "frame-dependent-simultaneity", "label": "Different frames need not agree on distant simultaneity", "description": "The passenger is at the train midpoint, with equal paths from the train’s ends in that frame. Using the same light speed there, the separately received flashes were not emitted simultaneously in the train frame, although the platform’s midpoint test said they were. This is more than an uncorrected signal delay."}
ID ground-lens
OLD {"id": "ground-lens", "label": "A shaped optical lens", "description": "A crafted glass surface changes the paths of light."}
NEW {"id": "ground-lens", "label": "A convex magnifying lens", "description": "A glass blank is ground into a convex lens. At a suitable close viewing distance it can enlarge the apparent object."}
ID heart-contraction
OLD {"id": "heart-contraction", "label": "The contracting heart drives blood onward", "description": "A simplified mechanical relation in Harvey’s circulation argument."}
NEW {"id": "heart-contraction", "label": "The heart drives blood out through arteries", "description": "The outward direction is placed beside the independently investigated venous return. Harvey could not observe the tissue connections later identified as capillaries."}
ID imitation-limit
OLD {"id": "imitation-limit", "label": "Lifelike motion does not settle what life is", "description": "The successful imitation leaves another question open."}
NEW {"id": "imitation-limit", "label": "Imitated motion does not establish a living animal", "description": "A gear-driven figure performs a movement. That alone does not demonstrate nutrition, growth, repair or sensation. The wider question of how living processes can be explained remains open."}
ID independent-instruments
OLD {"id": "independent-instruments", "label": "Other instruments can examine the same candidate event", "description": "Independent checks may share hidden assumptions; their design must be inspected."}
NEW {"id": "independent-instruments", "label": "Fictional result: separate instruments detect the same event", "description": "In this explicitly imagined trial, two differently designed instruments record a matching candidate event, and routine local calibration checks pass. Shared assumptions and common disturbances remain possible."}
ID independent-of-favour
OLD {"id": "independent-of-favour", "label": "A life not bought by favour", "description": "Fewer dependencies can make patronage less coercive; this does not prove poverty is always freely chosen."}
NEW {}
ID justice-as-debt
OLD {"id": "justice-as-debt", "label": "Justice as repayment", "description": "Reconstruct the proposal before testing it; making a position does not establish its truth."}
NEW {}
ID justice-question
OLD {"id": "justice-question", "label": "What makes an action just?", "description": "Socrates in Plato’s dialogue asks for an account, not merely a list of honoured people."}
NEW {}
ID lifelike-claim
OLD {"id": "lifelike-claim", "label": "Imitating an action is not reproducing every living process", "description": "A limitation of inference from appearance, not a denial that organisms have mechanisms."}
NEW {"id": "lifelike-claim", "label": "Living animals feed, grow and repair themselves", "description": "These familiar activities give concrete comparisons for a mechanical figure’s imitated motions. The list is not a complete definition of life."}
ID light-not-sufficient
OLD {"id": "light-not-sufficient", "label": "Brightness is not sufficient for heat", "description": "A negative instance rejects the easiest version of the conjecture."}
NEW {"id": "light-not-sufficient", "label": "Brightness need not bring comparable felt warmth", "description": "Bacon’s qualitative negative instance challenges a simple association of visible light and felt heat. It does not show that moonlight or visible radiation causes literally no heating."}
ID magnified-view
OLD {"id": "magnified-view", "label": "A magnified view", "description": "A lens makes a new visual relation available without a focal-length calculation."}
NEW {"id": "magnified-view", "label": "A magnified close-up view", "description": "Place the object suitably close to the convex lens: the changed light paths produce an enlarged apparent view without requiring a focal-length calculation."}
ID mechanical-procedure
OLD {"id": "mechanical-procedure", "label": "A rule-governed symbol machine", "description": "The conceptual machine makes procedure explicit without claiming that all intelligence is already explained."}
NEW {"id": "mechanical-procedure", "label": "A rule-governed symbol machine", "description": "Modern teaching comparison of implementation and abstract procedure: Turing’s 1936 machine model did not derive from Shannon’s later switching work. The finite rule makes symbol operations explicit without claiming to explain every kind of intelligence."}
ID microbial-growth
OLD {"id": "microbial-growth", "label": "Growth depends on arriving microorganisms", "description": "Observation and controlled access combine into a causal account of the broth contrast."}
NEW {"id": "microbial-growth", "label": "Broth growth tracks incoming microorganisms", "description": "The same air access with different dust access strengthens the causal account. The contrast does not rely merely on sealing one broth and leaving the other open."}
ID mobile-plates
OLD {"id": "mobile-plates", "label": "Continents move with larger crustal plates", "description": "The newer framework is not merely Wegener’s original mechanism declared correct."}
NEW {"id": "mobile-plates", "label": "Continents move with plates of rigid outer Earth", "description": "The mobile plates are lithosphere: crust plus rigid uppermost mantle. The newer framework is not merely Wegener’s original driving mechanism declared correct."}
ID moving-figure
OLD {"id": "moving-figure", "label": "A crafted figure can bow and turn", "description": "A particular automaton body awaiting a mechanism."}
NEW {"id": "moving-figure", "label": "Jointed figure parts and a supply of small gears", "description": "Material for a new automaton; the earlier sky mechanism remains a separate model to learn from."}
ID nominal-kinds
OLD {"id": "nominal-kinds", "label": "Shared names without separate universal things", "description": "A schematic nominalist alternative denies a separately existing universal; it does not deny all resemblance."}
NEW {"id": "nominal-kinds", "label": "Common concepts, not a shared thing outside the mind", "description": "A schematic nominalist alternative: common names and concepts signify many individuals, but no shared universal thing exists outside the mind. This goes beyond merely rejecting Plato’s separate Forms; Aristotle is not being made an Ockhamist."}
ID one-way-circulation
OLD {"id": "one-way-circulation", "label": "Blood flow constrained by valves", "description": "The work of an anatomical part supplies a directional constraint."}
NEW {"id": "one-way-circulation", "label": "Venous valves direct blood back to the heart", "description": "The observed work of the valves supplies a return direction, rather than merely asserting that circulation exists."}
ID one-way-valves
OLD {"id": "one-way-valves", "label": "Valves permit flow in one direction", "description": "A qualitative anatomical observation."}
NEW {"id": "one-way-valves", "label": "Vein valves allow flow toward the heart", "description": "Harvey’s qualitative tests on veins indicate the direction of return; they are not just an unspecified one-way gate."}
ID open-heredity
OLD {"id": "open-heredity", "label": "Selection with inheritance still unresolved", "description": "A fruitful account can retain an important unsolved problem. Later genetics must earn its arrival."}
NEW {"id": "open-heredity", "label": "Cumulative evolution with heredity still unresolved", "description": "Long-run cumulative change needs differences to persist across generations. If variation simply blended away, that raises a difficulty. The wider evolutionary account retains this unresolved mechanism rather than requiring a less-developed predecessor tile."}
ID part-and-function
OLD {"id": "part-and-function", "label": "A part understood through its work", "description": "Functional explanation asks why a part is present in an organism."}
NEW {"id": "part-and-function", "label": "An organ understood through the whole animal", "description": "Aristotle’s account of form directs attention to how an organ participates in this living animal’s organization. The earlier conceptual result now changes the explanatory question rather than merely authorizing observation."}
ID passenger-motion
OLD {"id": "passenger-motion", "label": "A passenger moves toward one arriving flash and away from the other", "description": "The events are the same; the passenger is not at rest with the platform’s midpoint."}
NEW {"id": "passenger-motion", "label": "At the train’s midpoint, the passenger meets one flash first", "description": "The passenger stays halfway between the train’s ends in the train frame. Relative to the platform, the passenger moves toward one arriving flash and away from the other. Equal train-frame paths therefore matter when applying the same light speed in that frame."}
ID platform-flashes
OLD {"id": "platform-flashes", "label": "Flashes from both ends reach the platform midpoint together", "description": "A verbal teaching setup: equal distances in the platform frame make simultaneous reception a test for simultaneous emission in that frame."}
NEW {"id": "platform-flashes", "label": "Train-end flashes reach the platform midpoint together", "description": "At emission, the ends of a moving train lie beside two platform marks, with the platform observer halfway between those marks. Simultaneous arrival over equal platform distances establishes simultaneous emission in that frame."}
ID population-pressure
OLD {"id": "population-pressure", "label": "A population–subsistence pressure", "description": "Malthus’s problem becomes available for later adaptation and criticism without requiring arithmetic."}
NEW {"id": "population-pressure", "label": "More offspring than available subsistence", "description": "Malthus argued that the capacity for population increase could exceed the growth of subsistence, creating pressure and checks. The claim is available for later biological adaptation and social criticism; it does not specify a just policy."}
ID positioned-sea-route
OLD {"id": "positioned-sea-route", "label": "A bearing chart with a located voyage", "description": "A map made for bearings gains an observed position; its distortions do not disappear."}
NEW {"id": "positioned-sea-route", "label": "A sea route marked with longitude", "description": "Clock comparison adds east–west position to the chart. Longitude alone does not supply a complete geographical position; latitude remains a separate determination."}
ID prism-spectrum
OLD {"id": "prism-spectrum", "label": "A prism separates white light into colours", "description": "A supplied observed effect, not an instruction to build or aim a laser."}
NEW {"id": "prism-spectrum", "label": "Prism colours persist; reunited colours make white", "description": "Newton compared a selected colour passed through another prism with recombined colours. The qualitative observations distinguish components of white light from the proposal that each prism simply manufactures new colours."}
ID redundant-code
OLD {"id": "redundant-code", "label": "Additional structured signals can reveal corruption", "description": "Redundancy can improve recovery without adding a new idea to the message."}
NEW {"id": "redundant-code", "label": "Structured redundancy can locate and correct the stipulated errors", "description": "The proposed scheme uses enough organized redundancy to distinguish the assumed error cases and recover the original message. Mere repetition or error detection alone would not guarantee correction."}
ID release-from-terror
OLD {"id": "release-from-terror", "label": "Freedom from the terror of death", "description": "Reconstruct the Epicurean inference about death and sensation without requiring agreement."}
NEW {"id": "release-from-terror", "label": "Freedom from the terror of death", "description": "The Epicurean material account meets the claim that suffering requires sensation. Reconstruct the argument against fearing a state without a surviving sufferer, while leaving grief and disagreement possible."}
ID shared-workshop
OLD {"id": "shared-workshop", "label": "A community owns a highly automated workshop", "description": "A speculative institution; ownership alone does not guarantee meaningful participation or fair outcomes."}
NEW {"id": "shared-workshop", "label": "A community workshop lets makers set tasks and pause machines", "description": "Speculative design: community ownership is paired with explicit maker rights to choose some tasks, revise operating arrangements and pause unsafe machinery. These rights still need institutions that make them effective."}
ID sterile-broth
OLD {"id": "sterile-broth", "label": "Protected broth stays clear; exposed broth grows organisms", "description": "A reconstructed contrast in microbial experiments, not a recipe for handling pathogens."}
NEW {"id": "sterile-broth", "label": "Air reaches both broths; only dust-exposed broth grows", "description": "A reconstructed microbial contrast: air enters both vessels, but the arrangement keeps dust and its microorganisms from one broth. That broth stays clear while the exposed comparison grows organisms. Not an instruction for culturing pathogens."}
ID textual-tension
OLD {"id": "textual-tension", "label": "A text relies on a distinction that its own cases unsettle", "description": "A focused interpretive problem, not a magic word that defeats any proposition."}
NEW {"id": "textual-tension", "label": "A critic calls copies passive, yet praises an identical later page as new", "description": "Authored interpretive example, not a quotation: an essay says only the original work can create meaning, yet treats an identical later page as intellectually innovative. The original/copy hierarchy is the named distinction under examination."}
ID universal-gravitation
OLD {"id": "universal-gravitation", "label": "One gravitation for Earth and heavens", "description": "A common dynamical account relates the previously separate domains."}
NEW {"id": "universal-gravitation", "label": "Newton proposes one attraction for Earth and sky", "description": "Continual orbital falling and Keplerian regularities motivate a common dynamical account. This pair reconstructs Newton’s proposal; a desire for harmony alone does not prove universal attraction."}
ID unstable-distinction
OLD {"id": "unstable-distinction", "label": "A particular textual division fails its own cases", "description": "The earlier context-sensitive reading helps identify a specific interpretive tension, not a universal license to ignore words."}
NEW {"id": "unstable-distinction", "label": "The original/copy hierarchy unsettles its own case", "description": "Borges’s context-sensitive reading helps identify a concrete tension: the critic’s later-page example grants a copy the creative role the essay denies it. This is a teaching comparison, not a claim that every text or distinction defeats itself."}
ID water-company-cases
OLD {"id": "water-company-cases", "label": "Comparable households, different water suppliers", "description": "Snow’s water-company inquiry provides stronger comparisons than a single dramatic pump story."}
NEW {"id": "water-company-cases", "label": "More cholera in intermixed homes using sewage-polluted water", "description": "Snow compared households served by different companies in the same districts. Those supplied from the more contaminated source suffered substantially more cholera than those receiving cleaner-source water. This was an observational comparison, not randomized assignment."}
ID waterborne-pattern
OLD {"id": "waterborne-pattern", "label": "Disease follows a water connection", "description": "A public, inspectable comparison helps separate a route of exposure from impressionistic coincidence."}
NEW {"id": "waterborne-pattern", "label": "Cholera mortality tracks a water-supply difference", "description": "An inspectable comparison of similarly situated households supplies a specific disease difference associated with water source. Confounding and the accuracy of records still require scrutiny."}
ID white-light-components
OLD {"id": "white-light-components", "label": "White light contains separable colours", "description": "An optical experiment changes the account of white light; a prism does not simply stain it."}
NEW {"id": "white-light-components", "label": "White light contains differently refracted colours", "description": "The inherited account of light paths meets colour-persistence and recombination observations. The glass redirects components rather than simply creating a fresh colour at each passage."}
